the supply of fruit vegetables and milk  to Europe schools is on the menu as EU  agriculture ministers meet in Luxembourg  the minister's will hear a progress  report on work toward new rules  governing the aid scheme for the supply  of these foods which have important  nutritional benefits for children milk  fruit and vegetable schemes were  introduced respectively in Europe in  1977 and 2007 the ideas to merge them to  increase efficiency and effectiveness  The ministers will also turn their  attention to the future of Europe's  dairy sector the European Commission  will present them with a report on the  market and if necessary set out possible  additional measures that could help the  sector the so-called milk package  assessed in this report came into force  in 2012 it was designed to cater for the  sector's long-term future once the quota  system ends in 2015 the council will  also examine the e use common  agricultural policy or cap and discuss  how Member States plan to implement key  parts of it following last year's  reforms Fisheries ministers meanwhile  will take stock of measures aimed at  ensuring that fishermen bring all their  catches to land and eliminating the  practice of discarding them at sea the  discard bans one of the key measures of  the Common Fisheries Policy which was  reformed last year and should apply from  early 2015 in this context the  Commission will brief ministers on  member states discard plans prepared at  the regional level with new kinds of  fish stocks to be subject to landing  obligations next year
 

